<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaitai Shinsho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kaitai Shinsho ('New Text on Anatomy') is a Japanese translation of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> edition of the German physician Johann Adam Kulmus's anatomical tables, published in 1774 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugita-genpaku">Sugita Genpaku</a>, Maeno Ryōtaku and their colleagues. The first full translation of a Western medical work into Japanese, it demonstrated the accuracy of European anatomy and gave a strong impulse to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>, or Dutch learning, in Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaitai-shinsho.jpg" alt="Kaitai Shinsho" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Babi Hijau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaitai_shinsyo01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 4 March 1771 the students of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">Rangaku</a> medicine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugita-genpaku">Sugita Genpaku</a>, Maeno Ryōtaku and Nakagawa Jun'an watched dissections of executed criminals at the Kozukappara execution grounds. Sugita and Maeno each owned the Ontleedkundige Tafelen, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> version of Johann Adam Kulmus's Latin Tabulae Anatomicae, and Sugita, struck by its accuracy, proposed translating it. They began the next day.</p><p>Only Maeno could read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and his vocabulary was limited; there were no dictionaries, and the interpreters were far away in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, so the work went forward in a way compared to code-breaking. In 1773 they issued a five-page flyer, the Kaitai Yakuzu, to test the response of the authorities. The court physician Katsuragawa Hosan put the finished book before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odano-naotake">Odano Naotake</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> artist from Kakunodate recommended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraga-gennai">Hiraga Gennai</a>, drew the figures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book, issued by the publisher Suharaya Ichibee, has four volumes of text in kanbun and one of illustrations. Maeno did most of the translating and Sugita put it into classical Chinese. Besides Kulmus, the translators consulted works by Bartholini, Blankaart and others, and left out the long footnotes that fill more than half of Kulmus's book. The volumes run from the skeleton and joints through the head and senses, the chest, heart and vessels, and the digestive organs to the kidneys, genitalia, pregnancy and muscles. Maeno is named only in the dedication; accounts differ on why he withheld his name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The translation advanced both medicine and the understanding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and helped the rise of scholars such as Ōtsuki Gentaku. Its translators coined words still in use, among them shinkei for nerve, nankotsu for cartilage and dōmyaku for artery, though many other terms written in Chinese characters soon disappeared. As a first attempt it had many mistranslations, and Ōtsuki Gentaku published a revised version, the Chōtei Kaitai Shinsho, in 1826. Late in life Sugita told the story of the work in Rangaku Koto Hajime, which shaped later writing on the modernisation of Japanese medicine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaitai Shinsho” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1774 Sugita Genpaku and his colleagues published the <b>Kaitai Shinsho</b>, a translation of a Dutch anatomical text, and by the early nineteenth century rangaku scholars had prepared intellectual foundations for the rapid modernisation of the Meiji period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
